@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — what a transmission says when nobody can fix the outcome
#
# One generalization, formed from seven attested claims in six transmissions,
# and its second clause from two more. It is the counterpart of the statements
# already in this directory rather than another of them: those describe how a
# transmission protects a claim, and this one describes the narrow class of
# cases in which it does not, and asks what distinguishes them.
#
# The counter-search against the holding was carried before the node was
# written, as SPEC §16 requires. It changed the statement twice, and what it
# found on the other side is in the notes. A second candidate formed in the
# same run did not survive it and was not written; the search that killed it
# is in examinations/searches.ttl, because a refuted candidate is worth more
# to the next run as a recorded search than as nothing.
#

pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Where no party can fix the outcome, both sides of the criterion are stated"@en, "Wo keine Partei den Ausgang bestimmen kann, werden beide Seiten des Kriteriums genannt"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission fixes the judgement of a single performance to a physical event that neither the operator nor the person operated on can produce at will, it states what the event would have to be for the performance to have failed and not only what it would be for the performance to have succeeded; where the criterion is instead an appearance announced to the participants before the session, the transmission states the success side alone."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:InuitForkSignOfSurvival, pmc:TungusSacrificeRejected, pmc:QizhiYouxiao, pmc:CizhongWuzang, pmc:ContestOfTwoNoaidi, pmp:NkasaTreeCutting, pmp:BengeCorroborativeTest, pmc:ClaviculaSignaAdventus, pmc:NeidanZhengyan ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The seven witnesses of the first clause, and what each of them fixes the judgement to. The fork of the Iglulik divination falls or stands: if it falls the sick person will die, if it stays upright she will live, and the reading is given before the fork is dropped. The kite spirit of the Manchu clan history eats the liver of an ox and the shaman must then leave his assistant; if he falls down without being able to keep on his legs, the sacrifice counts as rejected. The two Sámi practitioners who wish to know which of them excels paint two reindeer striking each other on a drum, and he whose reindeer has the victory is the better. The needling chapter of the Lingshu states what it is for the qi to have arrived as a rule over the vessel and then refuses the patient's own report twice in the same sentence: although he says it is as it was, although he says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The prohibition chapter of the Suwen pairs each depot with an interval and a bodily sign, so that a man struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by the sentence. The ordeal-giver of the Lower Congo cuts the tree with a formula that names both outcomes, the blade bending if the accused is a witch and entering if he is not. And the Zande poison oracle requires a second fowl told what the first answer was, which must come out the opposite way."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two witnesses of the second clause do instead, and why they are in the same node rather than in a file of their own. The Solomonic book tells the operator in advance what he will perceive when the pentacles are shown — noises and rushings, and six manners of coming sorted by the substance the spirits were made from, from great rains and thunderbolts to a gentle breeze. The internal alchemy announces its signs of verification in order, each introduced as coming next after the one before. Both are events nobody can produce at will either; a night in the open supplies weather, and a body under a regimen supplies sensations. What separates them from the seven is not controllability but that they are announced to the participant beforehand, and neither states what would have to appear for the work to have failed. The clause is in the statement because without it the first clause would be a remark about a handful of cases instead of a claim about a division."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was run against the compiled holding before this was written, and its first pass removed the word public from the statement. The criterion of the Lingshu is the hardness of a vessel under a finger, which is not public and is read by one person, and the criterion of the drum contest is the victory of a painted reindeer, which the source does not say how anybody determined. What the seven share is not that a bystander could check them but that nobody who has an interest in the outcome can produce the event; that is a weaker property and it is the one the witnesses actually carry."@en ;
    skos:note "Its second pass removed a stronger clause that the holding refutes: that a transmission stating both sides is one that abides by the verdict. The ordeal tree says otherwise in the same passage that supplies the witness. The recorder states that although the omens may be in favour of the accused the ordeal-giver proceeds all the same, that the results are not infrequently quite contrary to the omens, and that the belief is unaffected by the discrepancy. Two-sidedness is therefore a property of the wording and not of the practice, and the statement above claims only the wording. That limit is the most useful thing in this node, because it marks exactly how far a reader may carry a criterion found in a text."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search did not find, and this is what makes the first clause a claim rather than an observation. No transmission in the holding fixes a judgement to an event outside every party's control and then names only the success side. Nine candidates were read at the node and none of them does; four are named here: the maiden's declaration decided by a snake states that the challenger loses if he refuses the same test; the Andean confession test states that the confession is void and must be made again; the Māori hair-cutting names the cut scalp as the detector of a breach nobody admitted; the Sámi journey states an upper bound of twenty-four hours as well as the rule that the time tracks the distance. If a case exists it will be a plain refutation, and a reader who has one needs nothing from this graph to bring it."@en ;
    skos:note "The spread, stated so that the count of predecessors does not overstate it. Seven witnesses for the first clause, but the Lingshu and the Suwen are one transmission, so the count of transmissions is six: Iglulik Inuit, Manchu-Tungus, Sámi, the Chinese medical canon, Lower Congo and Zande. No route has been proposed between the first, the third, the fourth, the fifth and the sixth, which makes this the widest spread in this directory. The two witnesses of the second clause are a Latin grimoire and a Chinese internal alchemy, between which no route has been proposed either. The wide spread is the reason the statement is worth having and is also its own warning: a form that turns up in six unconnected places is a candidate for a structural necessity of the thing rather than for a shared insight, and SPEC §14 names that as one of the three sources of agreement independence does not protect against."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that fixes the judgement of a performance to an event no party can produce at will and states the success side alone — a divination whose text says what the favourable fall means and is silent on the unfavourable one, an ordeal that names the sign of guilt and not the sign of innocence. Falsified in the other direction by a set of announced signs of verification that states, in the same passage, what the absence of a sign would show about the work. Weakened, without being refuted, if the seven turn out to be selected by the reading: five of the six transmissions entered the holding through ethnographic corpora, and a recorder who found a stated criterion interesting is a plausible filter on what got written down."@en ;
    skos:note "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission; the queue it belongs to is derivable from the absence and is not to be written down. What a protocol would have to do first is name the population, and that is the hard part: the unit is a transmission and not a case, so the denominator is the set of transmissions that fix a judgement to an uncontrollable event, which nobody has enumerated."@en .
